\chapter{Getting Started: Installation, Build, and First Program}

This chapter takes you from an empty directory to a compiled, running MuDSL program. The C++ header and D module are individually self-contained; installation means making the chosen implementation visible to its compiler. Default builds need only the corresponding standard library. Native Lua embedding is an optional build described in Chapter 35.

We will cover the requirements, how to obtain and include the header, and how to compile a program directly with \texttt{g\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}+} or \texttt{c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}+}. We will then look at how to consume the library through CMake's \texttt{I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}E} target, and how to build and run the bundled examples.

Finally, we will walk through the first example program in detail and review the compile errors you are most likely to meet on day one. By the end you will have a working MuDSL program and the confidence to read the rest of the manual.

\section{Requirements}

For \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}, the language requirement is C++20. MuDSL relies on language features that did not exist before C++20. It uses concepts, both in real constraints and in \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}-expressions.

It uses class-type non-type template parameters, which let \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} serve as a template argument. It uses \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} lambdas and the brace of standard-library headers that accompany the modern language.

Any recent release of GCC, Clang, or MSVC that advertises C++20 support will do. If you are unsure of your toolchain, compile any small program with \texttt{-\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}0} first. Confirm the toolchain accepts it before reaching for MuDSL.

Default header builds use standard C++ facilities and the embedded \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m} headers. The byte-stream and language facilities also use spans, fixed-width integers and standard containers. Enabling \texttt{M\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}B\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}A} requires Lua headers and linkage supplied by the user.

For \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}d}, use a D compiler with Phobos and support for template mixins, \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h}, and CTFE. The module has been exercised with LDC, DMD and GDC. Its optional native bridge is selected with the \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a} version identifier and Lua linkage.

\section{A First D Program}

Save this as \texttt{h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}d} alongside \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}d}:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
import MuDSL;
import std.stdio : writeln;

struct BasicDSL { mixin DSL!(Pipeline, Operators); }

void main()
{
    auto result = BasicDSL.wrap(5)
        | pipe((int x) => x + 3)
        | pipe((int x) => x * 2);
    writeln(result); // 16
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
ldc2 hello.d MuDSL.d mull/mullier/*.d -of=hello
./hello
# Equivalent compiler invocations:
dmd hello.d MuDSL.d mull/mullier/*.d -of=hello
gdc hello.d MuDSL.d mull/mullier/*.d -o hello
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Supply the module directory through \texttt{-\allowbreak{}I} when it is elsewhere. The CMake installation includes both implementation files; its \texttt{I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}E} target exports the C++ usage requirements. Chapter 31 provides the D feature guide.

\section{Obtaining the Header}

The public header is \texttt{m\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} in the repository. A project that vendors MuDSL copies that file and the accompanying \texttt{m\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r} directory into its include tree, preserving relative paths. The CMake installation does this automatically.

There is no separate source file to compile, no static library to build, and no shared object to install for consumers. If your project already has an \texttt{i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}} or \texttt{t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}} directory, place the public header and its companion directory there. The optional MuLIB bridge has its own compiled-library target.

Once the header is on your include path, using it is a single line:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

That line pulls the entire \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l} namespace into your translation unit. Because the library is header-only, the cost is paid once per translation unit at compile time. There is no link step and no runtime library.

The \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e} guard at the top of the header prevents double inclusion within a single translation unit. The include guards of the standard library headers it pulls in prevent repeated work across the rest of your build.

\section{A Direct Compiler Build}

The simplest possible build is a direct compiler invocation. Save the following as \texttt{h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} next to a copy of \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct BasicDSL : dsl::DSL<BasicDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};

int main() {
    auto v = BasicDSL::wrap(5)
           | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x + 3; })
           | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x * 2; });
    std::cout << v << "\n";   // prints 16
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Compile and run it in one step with GCC:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
g++ -std=c++20 hello.cpp -o hello
./hello
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

With Clang the command is identical in shape:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
clang++ -std=c++20 hello.cpp -o hello
./hello
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

That is the whole build for a consumer. No \texttt{-\allowbreak{}l} flags, no \texttt{-\allowbreak{}I} flags beyond what your own layout needs, and no \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e}. The only mandatory flag is \texttt{-\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}0} or a newer standard.

Forgetting that flag is the single most common first-day problem. The compiler will reject concepts and class-type NTTPs with errors that can look bewildering if you do not know the cause.

If your project keeps headers in a separate directory, point the compiler at it with \texttt{-\allowbreak{}I}. For example, if \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} lives in \texttt{i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}}:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
g++ -std=c++20 -Iinclude src/hello.cpp -o hello
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Many MuDSL snippets construct class-type non-type template parameters. On some toolchains you may benefit from a larger template backtrace limit to keep deep diagnostics navigable.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
g++ -std=c++20 -ftemplate-backtrace-limit=0 hello.cpp -o hello
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

These are quality-of-life flags, not requirements. They make error messages easier to read; they do not change whether the code compiles.

\section{Consuming MuDSL Through CMake}

For projects that already use CMake, MuDSL ships a ready-made \texttt{I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}E} library target. The distribution's \texttt{C\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t} declares the target with a single line and exports the include directory and the C++20 requirement as usage requirements.

Consumers inherit these requirements automatically:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cmake}{}
\begin{MDCode}
project(MuDSL VERSION 1.0 LANGUAGES CXX)

add_library(MuDSL INTERFACE)

target_include_directories(MuDSL
    INTERFACE
        $<BUILD_INTERFACE:${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}>
        $<BUILD_INTERFACE:${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/..>
        $<INSTALL_INTERFACE:include>
)

target_compile_features(MuDSL INTERFACE cxx_std_20)
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Because the target is \texttt{I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}E}, linking against it adds no compiled object code. It only propagates the include path and the \texttt{c\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}0} requirement.

A consuming project written against MuDSL therefore needs nothing more than a few lines of its own:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cmake}{}
\begin{MDCode}
# Assuming MuDSL was added via add_subdirectory, FetchContent, or found
# through the installed package config.
add_executable(hello src/hello.cpp)
target_link_libraries(hello PRIVATE MuDSL)
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Linking \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L} is enough to bring in both the include directory and the \texttt{-\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}0} flag. CMake will not let you accidentally build the target with an older standard, because \texttt{t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{})} makes C++20 a hard usage requirement.

\section{Installing and Finding the Package}

The distribution also installs a package configuration. Downstream projects can then find MuDSL after it has been installed. The \texttt{C\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}t} exports the target under the \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:} namespace and generates a \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e} from a template:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cmake}{}
\begin{MDCode}
include(CMakePackageConfigHelpers)
configure_package_config_file(
    cmake/MuDSLConfig.cmake.in
    "${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/MuDSLConfig.cmake"
    INSTALL_DESTINATION lib/cmake/MuDSL
)
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

After \texttt{c\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l}, a downstream project can write:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cmake}{}
\begin{MDCode}
find_package(MuDSL REQUIRED)
add_executable(app src/main.cpp)
target_link_libraries(app PRIVATE MuDSL::MuDSL)
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The generated config runs \texttt{c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}s} and exposes \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}R} and \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}U\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}S} variables. Projects that prefer to set include paths manually can read those variables directly.

The config template is short. It includes the generated targets file and sets the include-directory convenience variables from the package prefix. Nothing in it is project-specific, so it works for any consumer that has installed the package.

\section{Building the Bundled Examples}

The source distribution includes an \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}} directory with two dozen numbered programs. Each one illustrates a single feature or integration. They are the fastest way to see the library in motion.

The examples are plain \texttt{.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} files that \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}"} and define a \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n}. You can compile any one of them directly:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
g++ -std=c++20 -I. examples/01-basic-usage.cpp -o ex01 && ./ex01
g++ -std=c++20 -I. examples/12-expression-templates.cpp -o ex12 && ./ex12
g++ -std=c++20 -I. examples/23-peg-definition.cpp -o ex23 && ./ex23
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

A convenient way to build them all at once is a tiny shell loop. This is also a handy smoke test for your toolchain:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{bash}{}
\begin{MDCode}
mkdir -p build
for f in examples/*.cpp; do
  g++ -std=c++20 -I. "$f" -o "build/$(basename "$f" .cpp)" || echo "failed: $f"
done
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The examples are numbered to roughly track the manual. \texttt{0\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e} matches the foundations of this chapter. \texttt{0\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}s} and \texttt{0\allowbreak{}8\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n} match the AST chapters in Part IV.

The \texttt{2\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}-} through \texttt{2\allowbreak{}4\allowbreak{}-} examples correspond to the PEG chapters in Part XII. Browsing the examples in parallel with the relevant chapter is an effective way to learn, because each example is small enough to read in full.

