\section{Walking Through the First Example}

Let us walk through the very first example in detail, since it establishes conventions used throughout the manual. \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} is reproduced in full below:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct BasicDSL : dsl::DSL<BasicDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};

int main() {
  auto v = BasicDSL::wrap(5) | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x + 3; })
           | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x * 2; });
  auto gt5 = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x > 5; });
  auto even = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
  std::cout << v << " " << (gt5(v) && even(v)) << "\n";
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Line by line: the \texttt{\#\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} pulls in the toolkit. The \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t} declaration defines an empty DSL that mixes in two features.

\texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}5\allowbreak{})} lifts the integer \texttt{5} into the pipeline. The two \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})} stages add three then double. \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d} constructs two predicates.

The final line prints the piped value and the conjunction of the two predicate checks. The pipeline yields \texttt{(\allowbreak{}5\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}3\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}*\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}6}, and \texttt{1\allowbreak{}6\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}5\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}\&\allowbreak{}\&\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}6\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}\%\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}0} is true, so the program prints \texttt{1\allowbreak{}6\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}1}.

Notice that \texttt{B\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L} has an empty body yet still has behaviour. The pipeline feature injects the static \texttt{w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p} method. The operators feature injects the static \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d} factory.

The free-function \texttt{o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}|} in the \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l} namespace handles the pipe. This separation — features contribute methods, free functions contribute operators — recurs throughout the library. Chapters 3 and 5 explore it fully.

\section{A Second Example: Expression Templates}

A second example worth building immediately is the expression-template demo, \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}. It defines a \texttt{S\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r} type that inherits \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}.

The type stores a \texttt{d\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} and exposes \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}. The arithmetic operators are then provided by the mixin:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
struct Scalar : dsl::DSL<Scalar, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  double v{};
  Scalar() = default;
  explicit Scalar(double x) : v(x) {}
  double expr_value() const { return v; }
};

int main() {
  Scalar a{2}, b{3}, c{4};
  auto expr = a + b * c - a;
  std::cout << expr.eval() << "\n";   // 2 + 3*4 - 2 = 12
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Compiling and running this confirms that the expression-template feature built a lazy tree from \texttt{a\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}*\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}a}. The call to \texttt{.\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} then reduced it to \texttt{1\allowbreak{}2}.

Chapter 15 and Chapter 16 explain the mechanism. For now, treat the example as proof that the toolkit compiles and runs on your machine.

\section{A Third Example: The PEG Engine}

A third example, \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}3\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}, exercises the heaviest subsystem — the Parsing Expression Grammar engine. It creates a grammar, adds rules with compile-time pattern strings, and parses a sentence.

Whitespace is routed to an ignore channel so it is matched but not reported as a token:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition();
auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \\t\\n]+">([](dsl::PEGMatch &m){
    std::cout << "skip_ws:" << m.length() << "\n";
});
ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;
auto &kw_if = grammar.add_rule<"if">([](dsl::PEGMatch &m){
    std::cout << "kw:" << m.value << "\n";
});
auto result = grammar.parse("if x1 + 2 then 40");
std::cout << (result.ok() ? "parse_ok" : "parse_failed") << "\n";
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Building this example is a good smoke test that your toolchain's C++20 support is complete enough for the whole library. The PEG engine exercises concepts, variadic templates, and \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} pattern compilation.

Parts X through XII cover the parser and grammar machinery in depth. If this example compiles and runs, everything in between will too.

\section{Common Build Problems}

When a build fails, the cause is almost always one of a small number of things. The first is the standard version. If you see errors about \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}, or \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s}, you have forgotten \texttt{-\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}0}.

The second is the include path. If the compiler reports \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}y}, the header is not where the compiler is looking. Add the right \texttt{-\allowbreak{}I} flag or fix the \texttt{t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} entry.

The third is an old compiler. A toolchain that predates solid class-type NTTP support will choke on \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} and \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} even with \texttt{-\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}0}. The fix is to upgrade to a current release of your compiler.

A subtler class of error involves template diagnostics. Because MuDSL is template-heavy, a mistake in your own code can produce a wall of instantiation traceback. Two habits help you cope.

First, build the smallest example that reproduces the problem in isolation, away from your real codebase. Second, read the \emph{first} error in the output, not the last.

The first error is usually the root cause. Everything after it is cascading fallout. The bundled examples give you a known-good baseline to diff against when something goes wrong.

\section{A Productive Learning Workflow}

Another useful habit is to keep a throwaway translation unit that you compile often while learning. MuDSL's features are independent, so you can grow this file one feature at a time.

Add the pipeline today, the operators tomorrow, and pattern matching the day after. Confirm each compiles before stacking the next. This incremental approach matches the manual's part structure and keeps feedback loops short.

If you integrate MuDSL into a larger project, consider wrapping it behind your own thin header. Do not include \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} directly from many places.

The library's own overview comment suggests exactly this. Define your DSL type in \texttt{m\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}, include \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} there, and have the rest of your code include only \texttt{m\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
// mydsl.hpp — the only file that sees MuDSL.hpp directly
#include "MuDSL.hpp"

struct MathDSL : dsl::DSL<MathDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {
    // domain-specific surface here
};
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

This localises the toolkit. It lets you adjust your DSL's surface without touching callers, and it keeps your build's include graph clean.

\section{Build Performance}

Build performance is rarely a concern for MuDSL itself. The header is modest in size, and the templates it instantiates are shallow.

The expression templates and parser combinators are the most instantiation-heavy parts. If compile time matters in a large project, factor heavy DSL usage into a few translation units rather than spreading it across many.

For typical use, the difference is imperceptible. A program that includes the header and uses a couple of features compiles in well under a second on modern hardware.

\section{Summary}

Put \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} on your include path and compile with \texttt{-\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}0}; default builds need no extra library linkage. With CMake, link the \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L} \texttt{I\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}E} target and inherit its usage requirements. For D, place \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}d} on the import path and compile it alongside the application. Optional native Lua builds add the Lua dependency described in Chapter 35.

Build a bundled example first to confirm the toolchain, then write your own \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L} subclass. The next chapter opens the black box of that base class.

You will see how CRTP and the feature mixins turn one line of inheritance into a fully featured language. By now, though, you should have a compiled MuDSL program running on your machine and a feel for the three-step loop introduced in Chapter 1: inherit \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L}, add your domain members, and express logic through the operators those features provide.

Everything that follows in the manual adds depth to that loop, one subsystem at a time. If your first program ran, you are ready to begin.
